DESCRIPTION:
Wireshark is a network protocol analyzer — a powerful tool used to capture, inspect, and analyze network traffic in real time.
It helps students, network engineers, and cybersecurity experts understand what’s happening on a network at a very detailed level.
◻	What is Wireshark?
Wireshark is an open-source packet analyzer that allows you to see what’s happening on your network — packet by packet.
It’s often used for:
•	Network troubleshooting
•	Protocol development
•	Network security analysis
•	Learning network concepts
◻	Features of Wireshark
1.	Captures live network data from Ethernet, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, etc.
2.	Displays packet details like source, destination, protocol, and payload.
3.	Supports hundreds of protocols (TCP, UDP, HTTP, DNS, ICMP, etc.).
4.	Filters traffic using powerful display filters.
5.	Provides statistical analysis of network performance.
6.	Cross-platform support (Windows, Linux, macOS).
7.	Graphical and Command-line modes (wireshark GUI, tshark CLI).
◻	Wireshark Architecture
+	+
|	User Interface (GUI) |
+	+
|	Packet Analyzer	|
+	+
|	Capture Engine (libpcap) |
+	+
|	Network Interface	|
+	+
•	Capture Engine: Collects live packets from the network using libraries like libpcap
(Linux) or WinPcap (Windows).
•	Packet Analyzer: Decodes packets and shows detailed information.
•	User Interface: Displays packets, filters, and statistics visually.
 
i.	PACKET CAPTURE USING WIRESHARK
1.	Open Wireshark application on your system.
2.	The main interface will show a list of available network interfaces (e.g., Ethernet, Wi-Fi).
3.	Select the interface connected to the internet.
4.	Click on “Start Capturing Packets” (blue shark fin icon).
5.	Perform some network activity (e.g., open a website, ping a server).
6.	Wireshark will capture live packets transmitted and received by your system.
7.	Click Stop (Red Square button) to end the capture session.
◻	Output: A list of captured packets will appear with columns like No., Time, Source, Destination, Protocol, and Info.
ii.	STARTING WIRESHARK

1.	Launch Wireshark from the Start Menu or Terminal.
o	Windows: Start → Wireshark
o	Linux: $ sudo wireshark
2.	Choose your desired network adapter (e.g., Wi-Fi 0, eth0, etc.).
3.	Once selected, packet capturing begins automatically.
4.	You can pause or stop capturing at any time.
5.	Save the capture file using File → Save As → .pcap format.
◻	Note: Administrative (root) privileges are required to capture live packets.

iii.	VIEWING CAPTURED TRAFFIC

The Wireshark window is divided into three panes:

Pane	Description
Packet List Pane (Top)	Displays summary of all captured packets with number, time, source, destination, protocol, and info.
Packet Details Pane (Middle)	Shows detailed protocol hierarchy of the selected packet (Ethernet → IP
→ TCP/UDP → Data).
Packet Bytes Pane (Bottom)	Displays the actual raw data of the packet in hexadecimal and ASCII formats.

You can click on any packet to expand its protocol layers and inspect its details such as:
•	Source and Destination IP address
•	TCP flags, ports, and sequence numbers
•	HTTP request or DNS query details


iv.	ANALYSIS AND STATISTICS & FILTERS

A.	Applying Filters
Wireshark provides Display Filters to focus on specific traffic types.

Filter	Description
ip	Show all IP packets
tcp	Show only TCP packets
udp	Show only UDP packets
icmp	Show ping packets
http	Show HTTP request/response packets
ip.addr == 192.168.1.1	Show packets from/to specific IP
tcp.port == 80	Show packets using port 80

Example:
Type http in the filter bar and press Enter — Wireshark will only display HTTP packets.
B.	Viewing Statistics
Wireshark provides multiple built-in statistical analysis tools:

Menu Path	Function
Statistics → Protocol Hierarchy	Shows percentage of each protocol used.
Statistics → Conversations	Displays communication between pairs of hosts.
Statistics → Endpoints	Lists all source and destination IPs.
Statistics → IO Graphs	Graphical view of traffic over time.

These tools help in understanding network utilization, protocol distribution, and traffic patterns.

